CLIP STUDIO - SETUP NOTES
==========================

Folder contents:
  clip_studio.bat      <- run this (double-click). Combines the old
                           clip_downloader.bat + face_track_crop.py into
                           one script with automatic first-run setup.
  face_track_crop.py   <- your editor script, unchanged.
  clipstudio_config.txt <- created automatically on first run (remembers
                           where you told it to save clips).

MISSING FILE - action needed
-----------------------------
face_track_crop.py does `import audio_enhance` at the top. That file
(audio_enhance.py) was not part of what you uploaded, so it isn't
included here. Drop your audio_enhance.py into this same folder before
running clip_studio.bat, or the editor step will fail with
"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'audio_enhance'".

What clip_studio.bat does on a brand new PC
--------------------------------------------
1. Asks once where clips should be saved (defaults to a "Clips" folder
   next to the script) and remembers the answer for next time.
2. Checks for winget, Python, FFmpeg and Node.js - installs any that
   are missing via `winget install`.
3. Checks for every Python package the editor needs (yt-dlp, opencv,
   mediapipe, numpy, moviepy, faster-whisper, Pillow, arabic-reshaper,
   python-bidi, demucs, torch) and pip-installs any that are missing.
4. Creates the clips/temp folders and an empty cookies.txt if needed.
5. Then runs the exact same download -> trim -> edit loop as before.

On every run after the first, steps 1-4 finish in a couple of seconds
since everything is already checked as "already installed."

Things worth knowing
---------------------
- winget requires "App Installer" from the Microsoft Store. Nearly all
  Windows 10/11 PCs already have it; if not, the script will tell you
  exactly what to install and stop.
- cookies.txt starts empty on a fresh PC - you still need to export your
  real YouTube/Instagram cookies into it yourself for logged-in access.
- The video encode uses `h264_nvenc`, which needs an NVIDIA GPU. On a
  PC without one, that write_videofile call in face_track_crop.py will
  fail - swap the codec to "libx264" in that file if you're moving this
  to a non-NVIDIA machine.
- faster-whisper and demucs run much faster with a CUDA GPU; they'll
  still run on CPU, just slower. Plain `pip install torch` on Windows
  pulls a CUDA-capable build automatically when an NVIDIA GPU/driver is
  present.
- First real run will still take a while the first time it needs the
  mediapipe face-detector model, a font file, or a Whisper/Demucs model
  - those are downloaded automatically by face_track_crop.py itself the
  first time each is used, exactly like before.
